book=r'''<section class="book-shell" id="founder-book"><div class="w"><div class="book-top"><a href="index.html" class="founder-back">&larr; Back to main page</a><div class="book-meta">Founder story · Brittnay Folkes</div></div><div class="book-stage" id="bookStage" tabindex="0" aria-label="Founder story book. Use the page buttons or arrow keys to turn pages."><div class="book" id="book">
<article class="page left cover" data-page="0"><div><div class="chapter">Founder story</div><div class="starfield">✦ · ✧ · ✦</div></div><div><h1>Why I built Universe City</h1><p class="tag">Brittnay Folkes · Denver</p></div><div class="page-num">01</div></article>
<article class="page right" data-page="1"><div class="chapter">Chapter 01 · 2016</div><h2>Losing my guiding light</h2><p class="bigline">I was 20, heading into 21, when my mom died suddenly.</p><p>What shook me first was not losing somebody who handled my appointments or organized my life. I lost my guiding light. My support system. The person I could call, fall back on, move back in with, love on and hug.</p><p>I lost the one person who had known me from the beginning of time.</p><div class="pullquote">I realized I would now be the person responsible for understanding myself as fully as she had.</div><div class="page-num">02</div></article>
<article class="page left" data-page="2"><div class="chapter">Chapter 02 · Adulthood</div><h2>Everything felt like so much.</h2><p>Finances. Expenses. Managing my time. Managing my goals and priorities. Knowing what to focus on.</p><p>I did not have clear standards for myself. I could act on a whim because I did not have a plan strong enough to return to.</p><p>There were no clear signs telling me which direction to go. No single plan for what adulthood was supposed to look like.</p><p class="bigline" style="margin-top:24px">None of these things were extraordinary on their own. Together, they felt enormous.</p><p>And I knew I could not be the only person who felt that way.</p><div class="page-num">03</div></article>
<article class="page right" data-page="3"><div class="chapter">Chapter 03 · Blank University</div><h2>Street University</h2><div class="photo-block"><img src="podcast-2018.jpg" alt="Blank University podcast artwork from 2018"></div><p class="photo-cap">Blank University · podcast era</p><p>At the time, I was creating from grief and trying to process what had happened to me. On my podcast, I used to joke that I had attended “Street University.”</p><p>It was my way of saying grief had hardened me. I had gone through one of life’s hardest lessons and came out feeling tougher, more guarded and, jokingly, a little more like a thug than the person I had been before it.</p><p>Blank University began as part of an old social media identity. The idea underneath it stayed with me: adulthood asks people to know things nobody formally teaches.</p><div class="page-num">04</div></article>
<article class="page left" data-page="4"><div class="chapter">Chapter 04 · Preparedness</div><h2>The question changed when I started thinking about kids.</h2><p>I wanted to work with kids. That made me think about adulthood from the opposite direction.</p><p>Instead of asking why I was struggling with certain things, I started asking what a kid should understand before adulthood so those things would not feel impossible later.</p><div class="pullquote">What do these kids need to have a firm grip on to be prepared for adulthood?</div><p>Time. Money. Health. Home. Work. Standards. Relationships. Responsibilities.</p><p>I started thinking in terms of preparedness.</p><div class="page-num">05</div></article>
<article class="page right" data-page="5"><div class="chapter">Chapter 05 · Systems</div><h2>I started studying the structure underneath everyday life.</h2><p>My mom was an engineer. She had a systems brain. I do not mean that she was quietly running an administrative system for my life. She was my mom.</p><p>But I recognize that systems instinct in myself.</p><p>For years, I kept asking the same questions in different forms: What makes a life easier to manage? What needs a schedule? What needs a rule? What needs to be written down? What needs to be maintained instead of repeatedly rebuilt?</p><div class="pullquote">I have spent the better part of ten years trying to understand how the pieces of everyday life fit together well enough to make them easier to manage.</div><div class="page-num">06</div></article>
<article class="page left" data-page="6"><div class="chapter">Chapter 06 · The stars</div><h2>The constellations became part of the architecture.</h2><p>My mom loved the moon and the constellations.</p><p>As Blank University became Universe City, I began using the constellations as structure. Each department has 12 folders. Each folder position reflects one of the 12 zodiac constellations, beginning with Aries at Folder 01.</p><div class="constellation" aria-label="Twelve folder positions"><span>01</span><span>02</span><span>03</span><span>04</span><span>05</span><span>06</span><span>07</span><span>08</span><span>09</span><span>10</span><span>11</span><span>12</span></div><p>Whether somebody relates to zodiac or astrology readings is beside the point. The constellations exist. Their existence became my devotion to the natural powers that be — my way of saying: I know I put this together, but I turned to the stars to make it so.</p><div class="page-num">07</div></article>
<article class="page right" data-page="7"><div class="chapter">Chapter 07 · Upbringing</div><h2>Law, order, dysfunction and the outlaw.</h2><p>Both of my parents were Libras. They were born on the same day.</p><p>I find that telling. I grew up around law and order, but outlaw energy and dysfunction visited our home too.</p><p>That contrast shaped me.</p><p>I can see it inside Universe City now: rules, standards, mediation, boundaries, records, law and structure — built by somebody who also knows what life feels like when structure is inconsistent, absent or simply not enough.</p><div class="photo-block"><img src="brittnay.jpg" alt="Brittnay Folkes, founder of Universe City"></div><p class="photo-cap">Brittnay Folkes · founder</p><div class="page-num">08</div></article>
<article class="page left" data-page="8"><div class="chapter">Chapter 08 · The name</div><h2>Universe big. City small.</h2><p>Universe City became the name when the idea got bigger.</p><p>I wanted the work to be able to hold more than one person’s schedule or budget. I kept thinking about the largest problems people talk about: hunger, homelessness, instability, education, work.</p><div class="universe-line">UNIVERSE <b>big</b><br>CITY <b>small</b></div><p>I would say, “What’s it going to take to create a better world?”</p><p class="bigline">“It’s going to take U in your CITY.”</p><p>The name became a way of taking something enormous and zooming in until it became personal enough to act on.</p><div class="page-num">09</div></article>
<article class="page right" data-page="9"><div class="chapter">Chapter 09 · Now</div><h2>I am 30 now.</h2><p>I have spent most of my twenties trying to build something that makes life easier to understand and easier to manage.</p><p>Universe City began with grief. It grew through questions about preparedness. It took shape through years of studying the ordinary responsibilities people are expected to carry.</p><p>And it became a city because I stopped believing the only useful way to think about big problems was at their biggest scale.</p><div class="pullquote">Sometimes the better question is smaller: What does one person need? What can we organize? What can we understand? What can we make easier from here?</div><div class="final-actions"><a href="index.html" class="primary">Back to main page</a><a href="index.html#departments">Explore the four departments</a><a href="https://deploy-preview-2--universecitystage.netlify.app/join">Apply</a></div><div class="page-num">10</div></article>
</div></div><div class="book-controls" aria-label="Book navigation"><button type="button" id="bookPrev">← Previous pages</button><div class="book-progress" id="bookProgress" aria-live="polite">Pages 1–2 of 10</div><button type="button" id="bookNext">Next pages →</button></div><div class="book-hint">Use the buttons, swipe on mobile, or use your left and right arrow keys.</div></div></section>
'''
s=s[:start]+book+s[end:]
# Remove old slideshow script if present and insert book script before closing body.
first=s.find('<script>\n(function(){\nvar port=')
if first!=-1:
    second=s.find('<script>',first+8)
    s=s[:first]+s[second:]
bookjs=r'''<script>(function(){const stage=document.getElementById('bookStage'),pages=Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('#book .page')),prev=document.getElementById('bookPrev'),next=document.getElementById('bookNext'),progress=document.getElementById('bookProgress');if(!stage||!pages.length)return;const reduce=window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;let mobile=window.matchMedia('(max-width:760px)').matches,index=0,busy=false,touchX=null;function maxIndex(){return mobile?pages.length-1:Math.max(0,pages.length-2)}function render(){pages.forEach((p,i)=>{if(mobile){p.classList.toggle('mobile-on',i===index);p.style.display=''}else{p.classList.remove('mobile-on');p.style.display=(i===index||i===index+1)?'flex':'none';p.classList.toggle('left',i===index);p.classList.toggle('right',i===index+1)}});const a=index+1,b=mobile?a:Math.min(index+2,pages.length);progress.textContent=mobile?`Page ${a} of ${pages.length}`:`Pages ${a}–${b} of ${pages.length}`;prev.disabled=index===0;next.disabled=index>=maxIndex()}function normalize(){mobile=window.matchMedia('(max-width:760px)').matches;index=Math.min(index,maxIndex());if(!mobile&&index%2)index--;render()}function turn(dir){if(busy)return;const step=mobile?1:2,target=Math.max(0,Math.min(maxIndex(),index+(dir>0?step:-step)));if(target===index)return;if(reduce){index=target;render();return}busy=true;const sheet=document.createElement('div');sheet.className='turn-sheet '+(dir>0?'next':'prev');const front=document.createElement('div'),back=document.createElement('div');front.className='turn-face front';back.className='turn-face back';const current=dir>0?(mobile?pages[index]:pages[index+1]):pages[index];const incoming=dir>0?pages[target]:(mobile?pages[target]:pages[Math.min(target+1,pages.length-1)]);front.innerHTML=current.innerHTML;back.innerHTML=incoming.innerHTML;sheet.append(front,back);stage.appendChild(sheet);window.setTimeout(()=>{index=target;render()},390);sheet.addEventListener('animationend',()=>{sheet.remove();busy=false},{once:true});window.setTimeout(()=>{if(sheet.isConnected){sheet.remove();busy=false;index=target;render()}},900)}prev.addEventListener('click',()=>turn(-1));next.addEventListener('click',()=>turn(1));stage.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{if(e.key==='ArrowRight'){e.preventDefault();turn(1)}if(e.key==='ArrowLeft'){e.preventDefault();turn(-1)}});stage.addEventListener('touchstart',e=>{touchX=e.changedTouches[0].clientX},{passive:true});stage.addEventListener('touchend',e=>{if(touchX===null)return;const dx=e.changedTouches[0].clientX-touchX;touchX=null;if(Math.abs(dx)>45)turn(dx<0?1:-1)},{passive:true});window.addEventListener('resize',normalize);render()})();</script>'''
s=s.replace('</body>',bookjs+'</body>')
p.write_text(s)
